# Machine-checked equivalence proof for the IGLA RACE MVP ternary classifier.
#
# Theorem: for ALL 256 8-bit inputs x, the generated multiplier-free RTL
# (IglaMvpTernaryClassifier, emitted by t27c from
# specs/igla/race/mvp_ternary_classifier.t27) produces exactly the same class as
# a golden model that computes every contribution with a real `*` operator and
# resolves argmax with real signed comparisons.
#
# WHY THIS IS STRONGER THAN THE ON-SILICON SWEEP.  The self-check loaded on the
# boards compares ten inputs against a table and checks a range invariant on the
# other 246.  This proof compares the FULL function against a multiplying
# reference, symbolically, over the entire input space at once -- and it does so
# on the artefact that is actually synthesised.
#
# WHY IT IS STRONGER THAN prove_ternary_mac.ys.  That proof covers one MAC of
# hand-written RTL.  This one covers a whole generated datapath: 24 weight
# decodes, three adder trees, and an argmax whose tie rule is a stated part of
# the specification.  It is therefore a statement about the COMPILER's lowering,
# not about one module a human wrote.
#
# Method: combinational miter over the two designs, SAT asked to find ANY input
# assignment where the outputs differ.  "no model found" == no such input exists.
#
# The DUT is regenerated from the spec rather than committed, so the proof can
# never drift from the specification it claims to be about:
#
#   ./target/release/t27c gen-verilog specs/igla/race/mvp_ternary_classifier.t27 \
#       > fpga/formal/mvp_classifier_dut.v
#   cd fpga/formal && yosys prove_mvp_classifier.ys
#
# -DSIMULATION excludes the generated test harness: the emitted `initial` blocks
# and `$display` calls are guarded by `ifndef SIMULATION` and become `$print`
# cells that have no BEL and no meaning in a miter.
#
# Refs #1959

read_verilog -DSIMULATION mvp_classifier_dut.v
read_verilog mvp_classifier_golden.v

prep

miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert IglaMvpTernaryClassifier mvp_classifier_golden miter_mvp
hierarchy -top miter_mvp
prep -top miter_mvp
async2sync

# -tempinduct, not -seq N.  The design here is purely combinational
# (`assign result = on_comb(x)`), so a single frame would already be exhaustive
# -- but writing `-seq 1` makes the proof's validity depend on a structural
# property of the design that nothing checks.  Add one register with feedback
# and a bounded check silently stops meaning what its heading claims, while
# still printing SUCCESS.  Temporal induction quantifies over ALL reachable
# states and costs the same 0.3 s here, so there is no reason to take the risk.
# See T115 in docs/theory/IGLA-FORMAL-RESULTS.md.
sat -verify -prove-asserts -tempinduct -set-init-zero -show-inputs -show-outputs miter_mvp
